.TH LOGGER 3 "2026-05-02" "4.3" "Logger Library Manual"
.SH NAME
logger \- internals and architecture of the Logger library
.SH DESCRIPTION
This page explains how the Logger library works internally.
For API usage see
.BR logger (1)
and
.BR logger (2).
.SH PRODUCER-CONSUMER ARCHITECTURE
The library operates with two threads:
.TP
.B Producer (main thread)
Formats log messages and pushes them into the ring buffer.
Any number of threads may act as producers simultaneously.
.TP
.B Consumer (writer thread)
Spawned by
.BR lg_init .
Reads messages from the ring buffer in batches and writes them to the registered sinks.
.PP
Because time strings are evaluated on the consumer side, the timestamp in a log message
reflects when the message was
.I written
to the sink, not when the producer called the log function.
.SH LOCK-FREE MPSC RING BUFFER
Since version 4.0 the library uses a
.B Multiple-Producer Single-Consumer (MPSC)
ring buffer implemented entirely with C11 atomics. No mutexes are used anywhere.
.PP
Key properties:
.IP \(bu 4
.B Atomic head
\(em
.I LogQueue.head
is atomic because both producers and the consumer read and write it.
Producers claim a slot with an atomic compare-and-exchange on
.IR head .
.IP \(bu 4
.B Non-atomic tail
\(em
.I LogQueue.tail
is owned exclusively by the consumer and requires no atomic access.
.IP \(bu 4
.B Cache-line alignment
\(em
.I head
and
.I tail
are placed in separate cache lines to prevent false sharing.
.I isAlive
is similarly aligned.
.IP \(bu 4
.B Slot sequence numbers
\(em each slot carries an atomic sequence counter.
A slot is ready to consume when its sequence equals its position plus one.
Releasing a slot advances its sequence by
.B LOGGER_RING_SIZE
so it can be reclaimed.
.IP \(bu 4
.B Batch processing
\(em the consumer drains as many consecutive ready slots as possible in one pass
.RB ( lgi_queue_ppr_batch ),
then writes them all with a single
.BR writev (2)
call per sink type to minimise syscall overhead.
.IP \(bu 4
.B PPR (Pop-Process-Release) separation
\(em popping a slot does not immediately release it.
Release happens only after the payload has been fully processed.
This prevents the producer from overwriting a slot the consumer is still reading.
.SH ADAPTIVE WAITING (BLOCK POLICY)
When
.B LG_BLOCK
policy is active and the ring buffer is full, the producer does not spin in a tight loop.
It progresses through three stages:
.TP
.B Stage 1 \(em plain spin
The producer spins without any pause until the spin count reaches
.BR LOGGER_WAIT_NO_PAUSE_MAGIC " (default: 128)."
.TP
.B Stage 2 \(em spin with PAUSE
The producer issues a CPU
.B PAUSE
instruction on each iteration (reduces power and pipeline pressure) until the count reaches
.BR LOGGER_WAIT_PAUSE_MAGIC " (default: 1024)."
.TP
.B Stage 3 \(em sleep
The producer calls
.BR nanosleep (2)
for approximately 60 ns per iteration (the kernel floors this to roughly 70-80 \[mc]s)
and loops until space is available.
.PP
Tune
.B LOGGER_WAIT_NO_PAUSE_MAGIC
and
.B LOGGER_WAIT_PAUSE_MAGIC
at compile time to match your traffic profile:
higher values tolerate longer bursts; lower values reduce latency under light load.
.SH DROP POLICY AND DEBUGGING
Under the default
.B LG_DROP
policy a message is silently discarded when the ring buffer is full.
To observe where drops occur, compile with
.B \-DLOGGER_DEBUG
or place
.B #define LOGGER_DEBUG
before the implementation include.
Debug output goes to stderr.
.PP
Benchmark result illustrating the drop/speed trade-off (1M logs, drop policy):
.nf
Is stderr?: YES
Log Policy: Drop
Dropped:    967413 logs
Elapsed:    65322363 ns
Per log:    65.32 ns
.fi
.PP
When stderr is attached, the consumer slows down significantly (roughly 4x).
Because the ring buffer is fixed-size, a slow consumer causes the producer to fill
the buffer and start dropping, which artificially lowers the measured time per log call.
Removing the stderr sink raised the measured cost from ~35 ns to ~55 ns per call
because fewer messages were dropped and the true queue contention became visible.
.SH SINKS
Up to
.B LOGGER_MAX_SINKS
(8) file sinks plus one reserved slot for the default log file can be registered per instance.
Each sink has an
.B LgOutType
that selects which formatted variant of the message it receives:
.TP
.B LG_OUT_TTY
ANSI-colourised output. Disable colours globally with
.BR \-DLOGGER_DONT_COLORIZE .
.TP
.B LG_OUT_FILE
Plain text, no colour codes.
.TP
.B LG_OUT_NET
JSON-formatted:
.BR "{\"timestamp\":\"...\",\"level\":\"...\",\"message\":\"...\"}" .
.PP
The formatter callback receives a
.I needed
bitmask and fills the corresponding
.I LgMsgPack
slots. The consumer then dispatches each slot to all sinks of that type in one vectored write.
.SH MULTIPLE INSTANCES
Each call to
.B lg_init
spawns a dedicated writer thread, so multiple instances run fully independently.
The global active instance (used by implicit-instance functions) is managed with
.BR lg_set_active_instance / lg_get_active_instance .
.B lg_init
sets the active instance automatically if it is currently NULL.
.PP
.B lg_destroy
does
.I not
set the active instance to NULL and does
.I not
free memory. The caller is responsible for memory management via
.BR lg_alloc / lg_free .
.SH LIFETIME AND THREAD SAFETY
.B lg_destroy
is safe to call from a single thread only when no producer threads are concurrently
pushing logs. Calling destroy while producers are active triggers a race condition
(Use-After-Destroy). The required ordering is:
.IP 1. 4
.B lg_init
.IP 2. 4
All log pushes.
.IP 3. 4
.B lg_destroy
(after all producers have finished).
.IP 4. 4
.B lg_free
(if heap-allocated).
.SH COMPILER REQUIREMENTS
.TP
.B C
C11 minimum (for
.B _Atomic
and
.BR stdatomic.h ).
C89 and C99 builds are possible by compiling to a shared library and using the
no-implementation header generated by the Makefile.
.TP
.B C++
C++17 minimum (for
.B inline
variable support).
